\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Sensitivity of ConflictRAG{} to the claim support threshold $\tau _s$. The default value of 0.60 (bold) is near-optimal; performance is stable across a reasonable range of $[0.50, 0.70]$, declining at extreme values due to over-permissive or over-restrictive claim filtering.}}{8}{table.caption.24}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:hparam}{{7}{8}{Sensitivity of \method {} to the claim support threshold $\tau _s$. The default value of 0.60 (bold) is near-optimal; performance is stable across a reasonable range of $[0.50, 0.70]$, declining at extreme values due to over-permissive or over-restrictive claim filtering}{table.caption.24}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Overall F1 vs.\ training set fraction (percentage of the 587 development examples). ConflictRAG{} is robust above 50\% of the training data; performance degrades gracefully with smaller annotation budgets. The NLI Conflict Detector baseline is less sensitive to training data volume because it uses only fixed NLI features without logistic policy training, confirming that the ConflictRAG{} policy layer's data efficiency is strong even at 25\% of the dev set.}}{8}{figure.caption.25}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:scaling}{{6}{8}{Overall F1 vs.\ training set fraction (percentage of the 587 development examples). \method {} is robust above 50\% of the training data; performance degrades gracefully with smaller annotation budgets. The NLI Conflict Detector baseline is less sensitive to training data volume because it uses only fixed NLI features without logistic policy training, confirming that the \method {} policy layer's data efficiency is strong even at 25\% of the dev set}{figure.caption.25}{}}
